> zerodivisors cd 2
the complex numbers have no zero divisors: |ab| = |a||b|, so ab = 0 only when a = 0 or b = 0
> zerodivisors cd 4
the quaternions have no zero divisors: |ab| = |a||b|, so ab = 0 only when a = 0 or b = 0
> zerodivisors cd 8
the octonions have no zero divisors: |ab| = |a||b|, so ab = 0 only when a = 0 or b = 0
> zerodivisors cd 16
zero divisors in the sedenions:
  (e1 - e10)(e4 + e15) = 0, although |e1 - e10| = |e4 + e15| = sqrt(2)
  so |ab| = |a||b| fails and the sedenions are not a division algebra (from dimension 16 on, every Cayley-Dickson algebra has them)
> zerodivisors cd 32
zero divisors in the cd(32) numbers:
  (e1 - e10)(e4 + e15) = 0, although |e1 - e10| = |e4 + e15| = sqrt(2)
  so |ab| = |a||b| fails and the cd(32) numbers are not a division algebra (from dimension 16 on, every Cayley-Dickson algebra has them)
> zerodivisors sedenion
zero divisors in the sedenions:
  (e1 - e10)(e4 + e15) = 0, although |e1 - e10| = |e4 + e15| = sqrt(2)
  so |ab| = |a||b| fails and the sedenions are not a division algebra (from dimension 16 on, every Cayley-Dickson algebra has them)
> (e1 - e10)*(e4 + e15)
  note: the sedenions have zero divisors: a b = 0 with a, b != 0 is possible, and |ab| != |a||b|
0
> (e1 + e10)*(e4 - e15)
  note: the sedenions have zero divisors: a b = 0 with a, b != 0 is possible, and |ab| != |a||b|
0
> (e16 + e17)*(e18 - e19)
  note: the cd(32) numbers have zero divisors: a b = 0 with a, b != 0 is possible, and |ab| != |a||b|
-2 e3
> abs(e1 - e10)
  note: the sedenions have zero divisors: a b = 0 with a, b != 0 is possible, and |ab| != |a||b|
sqrt(2)
> abs((e1 - e10)*(e4 + e15))
  note: the sedenions have zero divisors: a b = 0 with a, b != 0 is possible, and |ab| != |a||b|
0
> (e1+e2)*(e1-e2)
-2 e3
